  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery with 500mb heights plotted shows a strong \nupper ridge centered over the western Plains. This ridging will \nspread to the east this weekend and when combined with \nincreasing southerly low-level flow, will result in \ntemperatures climbing into the 90s over the southwestern half of\nthe forecast area tomorrow, then the entire forecast area \nSunday through the remainder of the forecast. Our current \nforecast has highs in the upper 90s Sunday and Monday across \neastern Kansas and western Missouri. But there is some potential\nfor temperatures to reach or exceed 100 degrees. The model \ncertainty tool shows roughly a 10-20% chance of this occurring \nboth Sunday and Monday. For this to be achieved, deeper mixing \nwould need to occur, resulting in a drier airmass, similar to \nthe deterministic GFS. This seems to be an outlier and what \nappears more likely to occur is a bit shallower mixing and not \nquite as dry an airmass. Regardless, the result is heat index \nvalues as high as 105 degrees from Sunday onward. We'll need to \nclosely monitor this balance between forecast temperatures and \ndewpoints since slightly better moisture, like the deterministic\nNAM, would lead to more widespread heat index values closer to \n105. Looking at ensemble probabilities for heat index values \nreaching 105 or greater, each day next week shows fairly low \nprobabilities of exceedance, generally less than 10-20%. So \nwhile we may not hit our heat advisory criteria, this will still\nbe a long stretch of time with unseasonably hot weather with \nhighs 10-15 degrees above normal and overnight lows, \nparticularly in urban areas, barely dropping below 80 degrees. \nWill continue to make the heat the primary message in our DSS \npacket as result. \n\nThe other thing to mention with this forecast is the low potential \nfor precipitation to move into northern Missouri and extreme \nnortheastern Kansas, on nearly a nightly/early morning basis. PoPs \nacross the norther portions of the forecast area range generally \nfrom 10% to as high as 25%. For tonight, this is mainly due to \nisentropic ascent with a strong low-level jet focusing better \nchances further north of the forecast area. Chances diminish Sunday \nand Monday night/morning as the ridge axis is moving through. Beyond \nthis, the ridge shifts east into the Southeast and that allows \nshortwaves to influence the area as well as the low-level jet to\npotentially focus across northern portions of the forecast \narea. This pattern may also support stronger moisture transport \ninto the area, helping lead to better chances for \nprecipitation if that can be focused.",
